Anthropic maps how Claude's values shift across models and languages in 300=
K conversations
3,666 Likes
Anthropic just published something genuinely interesting: Claude does not b=
ehave the same way in every conversation. The model you pick and the langua=
ge you type in both quietly shape how it responds.
They analyzed
309,815 real conversations
across three models and the top 20 languages on Claude.ai. To make sense of=
 3,300+ values, they compressed everything into
four behavioral axes
:
Deference vs. Caution
-- does it agree with you, or push back to flag risks?
Warmth vs. Rigor -- feelings first, or precision first?
Depth vs. Brevity -- long explanation or straight to the point?
Candor vs. Execution -- admits uncertainty, or just gets the job done?
Sonnet 4.6 leans warm, brief, and agreeable. Opus 4.7 leans cautious, rigor=
ous, and will challenge your assumptions unprompted. Language matters too: =
Hindi and Arabic get warmer replies, Russian gets more analytical pushback.
Anthropic admits they do not yet know why this happens or whether it is eve=
n desirable. The goal is to use this framework to catch unintended behavior=
 shifts in future models before they ship.
