<p>Anthropic just published something genuinely interesting: Claude does no=
t behave the same way in every conversation. The model you pick and the lan=
guage you type in both quietly shape how it responds.</p><p>They analyzed <=
b>309,815 real conversations</b> across three models and the top 20 languag=
es on Claude.ai. To make sense of 3,300+ values, they compressed everything=
 into <b>four behavioral axes</b>:</p><ul><li><b>Deference vs. Caution</b> =
-- does it agree with you, or push back to flag risks?</li><li>Warmth vs. R=
igor -- feelings first, or precision first?</li><li>Depth vs. Brevity -- lo=
ng explanation or straight to the point?</li><li>Candor vs. Execution -- ad=
mits uncertainty, or just gets the job done?</li></ul><p>Sonnet 4.6 leans w=
arm, brief, and agreeable. Opus 4.7 leans cautious, rigorous, and will chal=
lenge your assumptions unprompted. Language matters too: Hindi and Arabic g=
et warmer replies, Russian gets more analytical pushback.</p><p>Anthropic a=
dmits they do not yet know why this happens or whether it is even desirable=
. The goal is to use this framework to catch unintended behavior shifts in =
future models before they ship.</p>
</div>
